WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.980
DC has kept Batman's character pretty consistent
over the decades, but if one character is

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:10.410
going to lead more than 80 years of stories,
you're bound to get a few contradictions.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:15.969
Sometimes, DC has even outright lied about
Batman - through dead-end storylines, abandoned

00:00:15.969 --> 00:00:19.191
character quirks, and bizarre last-minute
retcons.

00:00:19.191 --> 00:00:25.050
When DC Comics and Grant Morrison decided
to title a storyline "Batman R.I.P.," they

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:28.240
had to have known they were entering some
pretty loaded territory.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.901
And okay, so the story isn't called anything
quite so direct as, say, "The Death of Batman"

00:00:33.901 --> 00:00:38.860
- but you can't really blame the readers who
saw the words "Batman R.I.P." and assumed

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:41.370
that Bruce Wayne's goose was about to be cooked.

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:42.800
They were wrong, of course.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.820
As it turned out, it was up to fans to pay
attention to the fine print.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:49.420
In 2009, Morrison told Comic Book Resources:

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:53.734
"This is an ongoing story, another chapter
in the life of Batman."

00:00:53.734 --> 00:00:58.868
And so Batman went on to survive the story
arc that was literally named after his death.

00:00:58.868 --> 00:01:03.300
Funnily enough, Bruce Wayne does seemingly
die in a comic published right around that

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.829
time - but not in "Batman R.I.P."

00:01:05.829 --> 00:01:11.060
That death occurs in another Grant Morrison
comic, entitled Final Crisis, in which a valiant

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:16.801
but unsuccessful attack on Darkseid ends with
the caped crusader reduced to a smoldering skeleton.

00:01:16.801 --> 00:01:22.430
While the image of an anguished Superman cradling
the body of his fallen ally was pretty powerful,

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:26.830
nobody really expected Bruce to be dead for
long - and so even this eventual pay-off turned

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:28.649
out to be kind of a dud.

00:01:28.649 --> 00:01:36.000
Grant Morrison's celebrated Batman run, which
began in 2006 and ran until 2013, was engrossing,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.067
cerebral, and genuinely inventive.

00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:43.201
Compared to most mainstream superhero comics,
however, it's not especially easy to follow.

00:01:43.201 --> 00:01:48.260
In an especially audacious move, the comic
references a hitherto forgotten story from

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:53.860
1963 called "Robin Dies At Dawn!," in which
Batman volunteers to play guinea pig in an

00:01:53.860 --> 00:02:00.468
experiment investigating the effects of isolation
and sensory deprivation, overseen by the mysterious Doctor Hurt.

00:02:00.468 --> 00:02:05.580
According to Morrison's run, while Batman
was in that state of heightened mental vulnerability,

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:10.369
Hurt implanted the words "Zur-En-Arrh" as
a subconscious trigger word to cause amnesia

00:02:10.369 --> 00:02:14.200
and confusion and generally render Batman
useless.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:19.319
Batman was two steps ahead as always, of course,
and preemptively created a backup personality

00:02:19.319 --> 00:02:22.959
that would arise if just such an attack on
his mind was ever successful.

00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:27.834
And that's how the unpredictable and garish
Batman of Zur-En-Arrh apparently came to be.

00:02:27.834 --> 00:02:34.329
The Batman of Zur-En-Arrh had first appeared
in a 1958 story that depicts him as an extraterrestrial

00:02:34.329 --> 00:02:37.189
hero entirely separate from the Batman of
Earth.

00:02:37.189 --> 00:02:42.140
It is thus implied by Morrison's comics that
the Batman of Zur-En-Arrh who Bruce Wayne

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:46.534
meets off-world is, in fact, an isolation-induced
hallucination.

00:02:46.534 --> 00:02:51.689
Even more remarkably, this whole Zur-En-Arrh
retcon opens the door to the possibility that

00:02:51.689 --> 00:02:57.439
many of the wackier sci-fi Batman adventures
of the 1950s were simply instances of Bruce

00:02:57.439 --> 00:03:00.909
tripping out on a journey across his own personal
dreamscape.

00:03:00.909 --> 00:03:02.299
Far out.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:08.349
By now, everyone's kinda tired of hashing
out the merits and criticisms of 2016's Batman

00:03:08.349 --> 00:03:13.379
v. Superman: Dawn of Justice: But one thing
that's especially hard to get over is Bruce

00:03:13.379 --> 00:03:18.269
Wayne's bizarre thirst for blood that's on
display throughout basically the entire movie.

00:03:18.269 --> 00:03:23.751
Batman kills a whole heap of folks in Dawn
of Justice, and, honestly, it's a real disservice to the character.

00:03:23.751 --> 00:03:28.939
Obviously, nobody's saying that superheroes
all need to behave like upstanding role models,

00:03:28.939 --> 00:03:34.090
but Batman's no-kill rule is fundamental to
who he is, and doing away with it makes Batman

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:35.600
himself feel like a lie.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:40.099
In the comics, Bruce's rule becomes a major
point of contention between him and Jason

00:03:40.099 --> 00:03:42.534
Todd, after the latter returns from the grave.

00:03:42.534 --> 00:03:47.739
While training with the League of Shadows
in 2005's Batman Begins, Bruce Wayne's falling-out

00:03:47.739 --> 00:03:51.060
with the group occurs when he's ordered to
execute a thief.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:56.489
In Injustice: Gods Among Us, the Joker detonates
a nuclear bomb in Metropolis, and Batman tries

00:03:56.489 --> 00:04:01.489
to stop Superman from extracting fatal revenge
on him, even under those extreme and arguably

00:04:01.489 --> 00:04:03.220
justified circumstances.

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:08.400
Those are three unrelated stories in three
different mediums that all agree on one thing:

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:13.629
The no-kill rule isn't an arbitrary choice
- it's the definitive aspect of who Batman

00:04:13.629 --> 00:04:15.799
is and how he sees the world.

00:04:15.799 --> 00:04:21.845
When you think of the 1966 Batman TV series,
you probably think of stuff like this.

00:04:21.925 --> 00:04:23.999
"Holy sardine!"

00:04:27.669 --> 00:04:32.860
Adam West's version of Batman certainly embraces
the sillier side of superheroes more than

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:37.400
most other versions of the character - and
so, to some degree, he can usually get away

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:38.930
with looking a little dim.

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:43.199
But even in this universe, Batman's foolishness
has to have its limits.

00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:49.400
The plot of the 1966 Batman spinoff movie
involves Batman's arch-enemies joining forces

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.199
and orchestrating a scheme to kidnap Bruce
Wayne.

00:04:52.199 --> 00:04:57.800
In order to execute this plan, Catwoman disguises
herself as Russian journalist "Miss Kitka"

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:00.301
and sets about seducing Gotham's favorite
son.

00:05:00.301 --> 00:05:05.430
During their various trysts, Catwoman never
notices Bruce Wayne and Batman are the same

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:09.801
person, and Bruce doesn't connect Miss Kitka
to Catwoman until she loses her mask in a

00:05:09.801 --> 00:05:11.389
fight later in the movie.

00:05:11.389 --> 00:05:16.840
In fairness to Selina Kyle, this version of
Batman's cowl covers basically his whole head,

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:21.590
and so it's not entirely inconceivable that
an otherwise intelligent person might not

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:24.198
notice the similarities between Bruce and
Batman.

00:05:24.198 --> 00:05:29.820
But the only thing concealing Catwoman's true
identity is a simple domino mask.

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:34.250
Catwoman and Miss Kitka are obviously and
unmistakably the same person, and the fact

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:38.870
that Batman can't figure this out demonstrates
a startling lack of intuition on his part

00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:43.318
- and might make you wonder whether he's really
the world's greatest detective after all.

00:05:43.318 --> 00:05:46.860
"Some days you just can't get rid of a bomb!"

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:51.250
There's no real point in harping on the impermanence
of death in superhero comics.

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:55.400
Truth is, if a major character dies in a comic
book, they're probably going to come back

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:57.110
at some point in the near future.

00:05:57.110 --> 00:06:02.330
Just a few decades ago, however, these infinite
resurrections weren't yet a widely-understood

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:04.659
attribute of mainstream comic stories.

00:06:04.659 --> 00:06:09.370
And because death tended to be more permanent
back then, the grim reaper generally left

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:12.090
the most important comic book characters alone.

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:17.650
So when DC readers voted to kill Jason Todd
in 1988's "A Death in the Family" arc, those

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:20.759
readers had every reason to assume he would
stay gone.

00:06:20.759 --> 00:06:26.810
And stay gone he did - for almost 20 years,
in fact, until Judd Winick brought the character

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:28.801
back as the new Red Hood.

00:06:28.801 --> 00:06:31.669
Still, let's not judge Winick too harshly.

00:06:31.669 --> 00:06:35.860
Seeing as how basically every other Batman
protege has been killed and brought back in

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:40.660
subsequent years, the second Robin's return
was pretty much inevitable - even if it was

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:41.999
a little disappointing.

00:06:41.999 --> 00:06:47.080
It's hard to put an exact number on how many
Batman stories have been told over the last

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:50.650
80 years - but it's not gonna be a small number,
that's for sure.

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:55.580
And as the key aspects of Bruce Wayne's past
have been revisited and reimagined by various

00:06:55.580 --> 00:07:00.979
writers, certain details have gotten mixed
up, turned around, and even swapped out entirely.

00:07:00.979 --> 00:07:04.598
After a while, this can get confusing… even
for Batman himself.

00:07:04.598 --> 00:07:11.849
During writer Tom King's 2016-2019 run on
Batman, Batman and Catwoman get serious about

00:07:11.849 --> 00:07:14.740
their storied on-again, off-again romance.

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:19.080
Occasionally, they reminisce about how they
first met, as couples often do.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:25.267
The problem is, Selina remembers their first
meeting as it happened in 1987's Batman: Year

00:07:25.267 --> 00:07:30.870
One, while Bruce recalls their initial encounter
as the one from 1940's Batman #1.

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:33.429
It could be that both stories happened in-continuity.

00:07:33.429 --> 00:07:37.469
Bruce and Selina haven't nailed down their
costumed identities yet when they meet in

00:07:37.469 --> 00:07:42.000
Year One, whereas Bruce is fully Batman during
their encounter in Batman #1.

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:46.860
But for that to be true, Bruce has to either
forget the Year One meeting, or not recognize

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:49.209
Selina during their second meeting on the
boat.

00:07:49.209 --> 00:07:53.729
It's all a bit tangled, but no matter how
you slice it, DC has lied about at least one

00:07:53.729 --> 00:07:57.050
aspect of Batman and Catwoman's early days.

00:07:57.050 --> 00:08:04.180
1999 was a pretty long time ago, so not everybody
will clearly remember Batman Beyond, the sci-fi-infused

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:08.199
animated series that introduced a brand new
Batman to the world.

00:08:08.199 --> 00:08:13.434
The cartoon follows Terry McGinnis, a high
school student living in the Gotham City of the near future.

00:08:13.434 --> 00:08:18.819
After the violent demise of his father, McGinnis
becomes a protégé of the now-elderly Bruce

00:08:18.819 --> 00:08:22.400
Wayne and assumes the mantle of Batman for
a whole new era.

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:26.880
Batman Beyond ran for three seasons, with
52 total episodes and a movie, to boot.

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:31.150
But fans didn't find out the whole truth about
Terry's parentage until years later - and

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:33.730
on an entirely different show, no less.

00:08:33.730 --> 00:08:39.040
In a 2005 episode of Justice League Unlimited
entitled "Epilogue," Terry McGinnis learns

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:44.020
he's actually the result of a complicated
plot set into motion by Amanda Waller.

00:08:44.020 --> 00:08:48.100
The one-time head of CADMUS had concluded
that the world requires a Batman, and that

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:53.520
she therefore needed to engineer a replacement
for the aging Bruce Wayne.Cue a complex scheme

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:59.390
involving genetic overwriting of Terry's dad's
DNA with Bruce Wayne's, via a fake flu shot.

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:04.650
"But when you're making a Batman, genetics
is only half the story.

00:09:04.650 --> 00:09:06.510
The rest is tragedy."

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:11.850
So, as it turns out, Terry McGinnis' father's
death did not technically inspire him to become

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:15.590
Batman - it was actually the death of his
stepfather that did that.

00:09:15.590 --> 00:09:20.210
And while Bruce Wayne certainly became a father
figure in McGinnis' life, neither of them,

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:23.250
nor the fans, expected it to turn out quite
so literal.

00:09:23.969 --> 00:09:29.020
Starting from the second arc of his Batman
run, entitled "I Am Suicide," Tom King began

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:34.590
playing with fire - especially when he deployed
Bane as the arc's master manipulator, arranging

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:38.067
various events and characters to engineer
Batman's undoing.

00:09:38.067 --> 00:09:43.250
Once the reader arrives at the finale arc,
"City of Bane," they get to see plenty of

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:48.360
the Flashpoint reality's version of Thomas
Wayne, who, simply put, is a huge jerk.

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:54.701
"City of Bane" also follows Bruce and Selina
as they attempt to repair their imploding romantic relationship.

00:09:54.701 --> 00:09:58.620
But one thing you won't find much of in "City
of Bane'' is Bane.

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:03.190
In fact, Bane functions as little more than
a secondary villain in the story, despite

00:10:03.190 --> 00:10:05.267
his name being right there in the title.

00:10:05.267 --> 00:10:06.267
So what gives?

00:10:06.267 --> 00:10:08.530
Well, as King later told Entertainment Weekly:

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:14.140
"Bane is the name of the supervillain, but
'bane' also means 'you're the bane of my existence.'

00:10:14.140 --> 00:10:17.270
Bane is also the feeling, the motivation of
Thomas Wayne.

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:22.067
That was supposed to be a pun and no one noticed
because it turns into the city of Thomas' bane."

00:10:22.067 --> 00:10:26.830
And in total fairness to the guy, a story
arc called "City of Thomas Wayne, the Angry

00:10:26.830 --> 00:10:31.034
Batman from Another Dimension" would've looked
kinda clunky on the cover.

00:10:31.034 --> 00:10:35.770
During his life, Stan Lee took a tremendous
amount of flak for allegedly taking credit

00:10:35.770 --> 00:10:38.850
for the work of Jack Kirby, Steve Ditko, and
others.

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:43.090
Whether the likes of Kirby and Ditko were
financially compensated sufficiently is one

00:10:43.090 --> 00:10:48.134
matter, but in terms of recognition, Stan
Lee generally gave his fellow co-creators their due.

00:10:48.134 --> 00:10:52.260
Savvy fans today know that they and their
colleagues deserve just as much credit for

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:55.680
Marvel's success during the 1960s as Lee himself.

00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:01.100
In other words, Stan Lee was never a grifter
on anywhere near the same level as Bob Kane.

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:05.650
As has been pretty much public knowledge for
a while, Bob Kane came up with the name "The

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:10.970
Bat-Man" in the late 1930s... and, well, that
was about the extent of his contribution.

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:16.370
Essentially, all the other elements that make
Batman Batman actually came from writer Bill

00:11:16.370 --> 00:11:23.668
Finger, who DC didn't acknowledge as a Batman
co-creator until 2015, decades after he died in obscurity.

00:11:23.668 --> 00:11:28.640
Kane flagrantly claimed credit for the character
regardless, along with no small amount of

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:29.910
money for his trouble.

00:11:29.910 --> 00:11:34.800
So there you have it - ironically enough,
we partially owe the existence of one of popular

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:38.420
culture's greatest fantasy heroes to an inveterate
scam artist.

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:39.500
Go figure.

